    "case_summary": "A 52-year-old man presented with sudden, pressure-like, substernal chest pain that started at rest and radiated to the left jaw, associated with nausea, dyspnea, and diaphoresis. Symptoms began approximately 6 hours before arrival. Two hours after intervention, the patient reported a new onset of fever and worsening chest pain that was retrosternal and sharp in quality, but not affected by positional changes or exertion.\n\nInitial examination: Patient was tachycardic, with no murmurs. No crackles were present on lung auscultation. Jugular venous pressure was normal. Follow-up examination: Temperature of 38.3°C, auscultation revealed a new pericardial friction rub located over the left sternal border.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\nInitial troponin levels were 14 µg/L\n\n- Imaging Studies:\n1. Initial ECG showed ST-segment elevations in leads II, aVF, and V3-V5\n2. Coronary angiography findings: acute thrombotic total occlusion of the mid-segment of the left anterior descending artery with TIMI 0 flow. Ventriculography demonstrated an ejection fraction of 55% to 60%\n3. Second ECG: complete resolution of ST-segment elevation in V2-V3 with persistence of ST-segment elevation in II, III, aVF, and V4-V6 without reciprocal changes\n4. Third ECG (24 hours later): diffuse ST-segment elevation on II, III, aVF, and V3-V5 accompanied by PR-segment depression in I and II and PR-segment elevation in aVR without reciprocal changes\n5. Transthoracic echocardiogram: no evidence of pericardial effusion, no wall motion abnormalities, ejection fraction of 55% to 60%",
